(window.DOM_CHAPTERS=window.DOM_CHAPTERS||{})["cardiac-output"]="<h1>Cardiac Output and the Frank–Starling Law</h1>\n<h2 id=\"1-what-is-cardiac-output-and-why-does-it-matter\"><span class=\"sec-num\">1.</span> What is cardiac output, and why does it matter?</h2>\n<p><strong>Cardiac output (CO)</strong> is the volume of blood <strong>one ventricle</strong> pumps out <strong>each minute</strong>. It is how much blood the heart delivers to the body, not how much it ejects in one beat.</p>\n<p>It depends on two things:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Heart rate (HR):</strong> beats per minute.</li>\n<li><strong><span data-term=\"stroke-volume\">Stroke volume</span> (SV):</strong> the blood ejected by one ventricle in <strong>one beat</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Cardiac output</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"CO = HR × SV\"><span class=\"eq-layout\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">CO</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">=</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">HR</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">×</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">SV</span></span></span><span class=\"equation-example\">Example: 75 beats/min × 70 mL/beat = <strong>5,250 mL/min = 5.25 L/min</strong></span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Why multiply?</strong> Each beat sends out one stroke volume. 75 beats of 70 mL each is 75 × 70 mL in a minute.</p>\n<p>At rest, cardiac output is about <strong>5–5.5 L/min</strong> in an adult. That is roughly the whole blood volume pumped round once a minute. It varies with body size and activity.</p>\n<h3>Why must the right and left ventricles pump the same amount?</h3>\n<p>The two circulations are joined <strong>in series</strong>. Everything the right ventricle pumps into the lungs comes back to the left ventricle. If the right ventricle pumped even slightly more each minute than the left, blood would pile up in the lungs. So <strong>over time the two outputs must be equal</strong>, even though the left ventricle works at a much higher pressure. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cv-introduction?section=2-the-two-major-circulatory-pathways\">→ Introduction, §2</a>)</em></p>\n<h3>Cardiac index: allowing for body size</h3>\n<p>A bigger person needs more blood flow than a smaller one. <strong>Cardiac index (CI)</strong> divides cardiac output by <strong>body surface area (BSA)</strong>, so people of different sizes can be compared fairly.</p>\n<div class=\"equation-block\"><span class=\"equation-label\">Cardiac index</span><span class=\"equation-formula\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"CI = (CO) divided by (BSA)\"><span class=\"eq-layout\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">CI</span><span class=\"eq-token eq-operator\">=</span><span class=\"eq-fraction\"><span class=\"eq-numerator\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">CO</span></span><span class=\"eq-denominator\"><span class=\"eq-token eq-identifier\">BSA</span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"equation-example\">For CO = 5.25 L/min and BSA = 1.75 m²: <strong>CI = 3.0 L/min/m²</strong>.</span></div>\n\n<h2 id=\"2-why-can-changing-heart-rate-alter-cardiac-output\"><span class=\"sec-num\">2.</span> Why can changing heart rate alter cardiac output?</h2>\n<p>From CO = HR × SV, a faster heart rate raises cardiac output <strong>only if stroke volume holds up</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Sympathetic nerves usually raise both.</strong> They speed the <span data-term=\"sa-node\">SA node</span> (positive <strong><span data-term=\"chronotropy\">chronotropy</span></strong>) and make each contraction stronger (positive <strong><span data-term=\"inotropy\">inotropy</span></strong>). So in exercise, HR and SV rise together, and CO rises a lot.</p>\n<p><strong>But a very fast heart can pump less.</strong> The ventricles fill during diastole. As heart rate rises, diastole shortens much more than systole. At very high rates there is too little time to fill, so each beat ejects less.</p>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> 75 beats/min × 70 mL = <strong>5.25 L/min</strong>. 130 beats/min × 35 mL = <strong>4.55 L/min</strong>. The heart is beating faster but pumping less.</p>\n<div class=\"co-impact-strip\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Two determinants of cardiac output\"><span><strong>HR ↑</strong><small>More beats per minute</small></span><span><strong>SV ↓ markedly</strong><small>Less blood each beat</small></span><span><strong>CO may ↓</strong><small>Depends on their product</small></span></div>\n\n<p><strong>Rule:</strong> never predict cardiac output from heart rate alone. Always ask what happened to stroke volume.</p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"3\" data-part=\"Sections 1–2\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 1–2 · 3 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 1–2</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> A heart beats 80 times a minute and ejects 60 mL each beat. What is the cardiac output?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. 1.3 L/min, because 80 ÷ 60 = 1.3</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. 4.8 L/min, because 80 × 60 mL = 4,800 mL/min</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. 14.0 L/min, because 80 + 60 = 140</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. 48.0 L/min, because 80 × 60 = 48,000 mL/min</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cardiac-output:ed5f379a\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> CO = HR × SV = 80 × 60 mL = 4,800 mL/min = 4.8 L/min.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why must right and left ventricular outputs be equal over time?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Both ventricles generate exactly the same pressure</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The two circulations are in series, so unequal outputs would pile up blood</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. The septum lets blood mix until both ventricles hold the same volume</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The same nerve fires both ventricles at the same strength</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cardiac-output:4d83d04e\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> All blood leaving the right ventricle returns to the left. Unequal outputs would move blood steadily from one circulation into the other.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Heart rate rises from 75 to 130 beats/min, while stroke volume falls from 70 to 35 mL. What happens to cardiac output?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It rises, because the heart is beating faster</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It stays the same, because the two changes cancel out</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It falls, because SV fell proportionally more than HR rose</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It cannot change, because CO depends only on venous return</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cardiac-output:29a3086c\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> 75 × 70 = 5.25 L/min; 130 × 35 = 4.55 L/min. Shorter filling time cut SV by half, which outweighed the faster rate.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"3-what-determines-stroke-volume\"><span class=\"sec-num\">3.</span> What determines stroke volume?</h2>\n<p>Stroke volume is what the ventricle starts with minus what it leaves behind: <strong>SV = <span data-term=\"edv\">EDV</span> − <span data-term=\"esv\">ESV</span></strong> (end-diastolic volume minus end-systolic volume). <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-cycle?section=7-how-much-blood-does-the-ventricle-pump\">→ Cardiac Cycle, §7</a>)</em></p>\n<p>Three things decide it:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Factor</th>\n  <th>The question it answers</th>\n  <th>If it rises (others unchanged)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Preload</strong></td>\n  <td>How stretched is the ventricle before it contracts?</td>\n  <td>SV rises (within limits)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Afterload</strong></td>\n  <td>How hard is it to push the blood out?</td>\n  <td>SV falls</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Contractility</strong></td>\n  <td>How strongly can the muscle contract at the same stretch?</td>\n  <td>SV rises</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<h3>Preload: the starting stretch</h3>\n<p><strong>Preload</strong> is how stretched the ventricular muscle is <strong>at the end of filling</strong>, just before it contracts. The more blood that fills the ventricle, the more it stretches. So <strong>end-diastolic volume (EDV)</strong> is the usual way to estimate preload.</p>\n<p><strong>Volume and pressure are not the same.</strong> A stiff ventricle can have a high filling pressure while holding little blood, so filling pressure can mislead.</p>\n<h3>Afterload: the load against ejection</h3>\n<p><strong>Afterload</strong> is what the ventricle must push against to eject blood. For the left ventricle, it is mainly the <strong>pressure in the aorta</strong>: the aortic valve only opens once ventricular pressure exceeds it. A higher aortic pressure means more of each contraction is spent just reaching that pressure, so <strong>less blood is ejected</strong> and more is left behind (higher ESV).</p>\n<h3>Contractility: how strong the muscle is at a given stretch</h3>\n<p><strong>Contractility</strong> (<span data-term=\"inotropy\">inotropy</span>) is how forcefully the muscle contracts <strong>at the same stretch and the same load</strong>. It depends mainly on how much calcium reaches the contractile proteins in each beat. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/cardiac-muscle?section=4-excitation-contraction-coupling-from-electrical-signal-to-force\">→ Cardiac Muscle, §4</a>)</em></p>\n<p><strong>Key distinction:</strong> a ventricle that contracts harder <strong>because it was stretched more</strong> is using the Frank–Starling mechanism. That is <strong>not</strong> a rise in contractility. A rise in contractility means a stronger contraction <strong>without</strong> extra stretch.</p>\n<h2 id=\"4-frank-starling-law-why-does-more-filling-produce-more-ejection\"><span class=\"sec-num\">4.</span> Frank–Starling law: why does more filling produce more ejection?</h2>\n<p><strong>The law:</strong> within normal limits, <strong>the more the ventricle is filled, the more it ejects</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Why does stretched muscle contract harder?</strong> Two reasons:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Better overlap of the filaments.</strong> At normal resting volumes, the heart's <span data-term=\"sarcomere\">sarcomeres</span> are shorter than their best length. Stretching them brings actin and myosin closer to the length where the most cross-bridges can form.</li>\n<li><strong>The muscle becomes more sensitive to calcium.</strong> Stretch makes the contractile proteins respond more strongly to the same amount of calcium.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"co-cause-flow\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Frank Starling mechanism\"><span>Venous return ↑</span><b>→</b><span>EDV and fibre stretch ↑</span><b>→</b><span>Force generated ↑</span><b>→</b><span>SV ↑</span></div>\n\n<p><strong>What is it for? Matching output to input, beat by beat.</strong> If more blood returns to a ventricle, it stretches more and ejects more, so blood doesn't pile up. This is also what keeps the two ventricles in step: if the right ventricle sends a little extra to the lungs, the left ventricle fills a little more and pumps it out. No nerve signal is needed. It is built into the muscle.</p>\n<p><strong>Does more filling always mean more output?</strong> No. At high filling, the curve <strong>flattens</strong>: extra volume adds less and less SV. A badly failing ventricle may sit on a flat curve, where extra filling mainly raises pressure behind the heart and causes congestion.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> The lecture's curve shows a dashed falling limb at extreme stretch. That is a teaching extension: an intact human heart does not normally work on a falling limb.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"5-how-do-we-read-frank-starling-curves\"><span class=\"sec-num\">5.</span> How do we read Frank–Starling curves?</h2>\n<p>A Frank–Starling curve plots <strong>stroke volume</strong> (or cardiac output) against <strong>filling</strong> (EDV). There are two ways the point can move.</p>\n<div class=\"view-switch\">\n<figure class=\"learning-figure cardiac-output-figure static-view\"><figcaption>Frank–Starling curves at a glance</figcaption><svg viewBox=\"0 0 640 295\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Schematic stroke-volume versus end-diastolic-volume graph showing increased, normal and reduced cardiac contractility curves.\"><line x1=\"85\" y1=\"20\" x2=\"85\" y2=\"238\" stroke=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".45\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><line x1=\"85\" y1=\"238\" x2=\"600\" y2=\"238\" stroke=\"currentColor\" opacity=\".45\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><path d=\"M99 229 C168 100 285 57 580 55\" stroke=\"#2863dd\" stroke-width=\"4.5\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M99 232 C188 181 275 148 580 148\" stroke=\"#16a085\" stroke-width=\"4.5\" fill=\"none\"/><path d=\"M99 235 C205 222 320 208 580 207\" stroke=\"#c39431\" stroke-width=\"4.5\" fill=\"none\"/><g fill=\"currentColor\" font-size=\"13\"><text x=\"190\" y=\"273\">End-diastolic volume (preload) →</text><text x=\"22\" y=\"130\" transform=\"rotate(-90,22,130)\">Stroke volume</text><text x=\"387\" y=\"40\">Greater contractility</text><text x=\"425\" y=\"138\">Reference</text><text x=\"432\" y=\"196\">Lower contractility</text></g></svg><p><strong>Read across one curve:</strong> changing EDV changes stroke volume by the Frank–Starling mechanism. <strong>Compare curves vertically:</strong> at the same EDV, greater intrinsic contractility gives greater SV (with afterload held constant). Schematic; not patient-specific measurements.</p></figure>\n<figure class=\"learning-figure teaching-widget\" data-widget=\"starling-explorer\" data-desc=\"sr\"><figcaption>Frank–Starling explorer</figcaption><p>Replaces the static curves. Three curves: low, normal and high contractility. A slider sets EDV, and the dot slides along the current curve. Buttons switch contractility, and the dot jumps up or down to another curve at the same EDV. A readout shows EDV, SV and CO (at 70 beats/min), and one sentence says which kind of change just happened.</p></figure>\n</div>\n\n<h3>Moving along the same curve: a change in preload</h3>\n<p>Someone who has been standing lies down. Blood stops pooling in the legs and returns to the heart, so EDV rises. The point moves <strong>right and up along the same curve</strong>: more filling, more SV. Contractility has not changed.</p>\n<h3>Jumping to another curve: a change in contractility</h3>\n<p>Two beats have <strong>the same EDV</strong>, but before the second one, sympathetic nerves raise calcium entry. The second beat ejects more <strong>without any extra stretch</strong>: the point jumps to a <strong>higher curve</strong>. A weakened heart muscle sits on a <strong>lower curve</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Careful:</strong> a higher <span data-term=\"afterload\">afterload</span> also lowers the curve. So a lower curve doesn't prove that contractility has fallen, unless afterload is the same.</p>\n<h3>Two lecture terms: heterometric and homometric</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Term</th>\n  <th>Meaning (break it down)</th>\n  <th>Example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Heterometric</strong> regulation</td>\n  <td><em>hetero</em> = different, <em>metric</em> = length: output changes because the <strong>starting length changes</strong></td>\n  <td>More <span data-term=\"venous-return\">venous return</span> → more stretch → more SV (Frank–Starling)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td><strong>Homometric</strong> regulation</td>\n  <td><em>homo</em> = same: output changes at the <strong>same starting length</strong></td>\n  <td>Sympathetic stimulation → stronger beat at the same EDV</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"4\" data-part=\"Sections 3–5\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 3–5 · 4 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 3–5</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Two beats have the same EDV, but the second ejects more blood after sympathetic stimulation. What changed?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Preload rose, moving the beat along the same curve</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Contractility rose, shifting the beat to a higher curve</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Afterload rose, making ejection easier for the ventricle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Venous return rose, stretching the fibres more</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cardiac-output:7dbfacab\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> With EDV unchanged, the stretch is the same. A bigger SV at the same stretch means stronger contraction: a higher curve (homometric regulation).</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> Why does a stretched ventricle contract more forcefully?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Stretch opens more gap junctions between the cells</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Stretch triggers a sympathetic reflex from the atria</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Stretch brings filaments to better overlap and raises calcium sensitivity</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Stretch lowers aortic pressure so ejection is easier</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cardiac-output:50e55ce5\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Normal resting sarcomeres are shorter than optimal, so stretch improves actin–myosin overlap, and the proteins also respond more strongly to calcium.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> Aortic pressure suddenly rises, with no change in filling or contractility. What happens to the next beat?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. SV rises, because the ventricle contracts against more force</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. SV is unchanged, because preload and contractility are the same</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. SV falls and more blood is left behind in the ventricle</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. SV falls because the ventricle fills less during diastole</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cardiac-output:36c211a0\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Higher afterload means more of the contraction is spent reaching aortic pressure, so less is ejected and ESV rises.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Someone who has stood still for a long time lies down. Where does their heart move on the Frank–Starling graph?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Along the same curve, to the right and up</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. To a higher curve, at the same EDV</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Along the same curve, to the left and down</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. To a lower curve, at the same EDV</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cardiac-output:d196d006\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> Lying down returns pooled blood to the heart, raising EDV. More filling moves the point along the same curve: preload changed, not contractility.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"6-what-raises-or-lowers-end-diastolic-volume\"><span class=\"sec-num\">6.</span> What raises or lowers end-diastolic volume?</h2>\n<p>EDV depends on <strong>how much blood comes back</strong>, <strong>how long the ventricle has to fill</strong> and <strong>how easily it stretches</strong>.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Raises EDV</th>\n  <th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>More blood volume</td>\n  <td>More blood available to return</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Veins tightening (venous tone ↑)</td>\n  <td>Squeezes blood out of the veins towards the heart</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Skeletal muscle pump</td>\n  <td>Contracting muscles squeeze veins; valves keep blood moving towards the heart</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Breathing (respiratory pump)</td>\n  <td>Breathing in lowers chest pressure and draws blood in</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Strong atrial contraction</td>\n  <td>Tops up the ventricle at the end of filling</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Longer filling time (slower heart rate)</td>\n  <td>More time for blood to enter</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n  <th>Lowers EDV</th>\n  <th>Why</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n  <td>Blood loss</td>\n  <td>Less blood to return</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Standing still</td>\n  <td>Gravity pools blood in the leg veins</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>Fluid around the heart (<span data-term=\"tamponade\">tamponade</span>)</td>\n  <td>Pressure from outside stops the ventricle expanding</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>A stiff ventricle</td>\n  <td>It resists stretching, so it fills less</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n  <td>A very fast heart rate</td>\n  <td>Too little time to fill</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The lecture also lists <strong>heart attack (myocardial infarction)</strong> and <strong>endomyocardial fibrosis (EMF)</strong>. Both damage or stiffen the ventricle wall, so filling and pumping are affected, and the effect on EDV depends on the disease and its stage.</p>\n<h2 id=\"7-what-changes-contractility\"><span class=\"sec-num\">7.</span> What changes contractility?</h2>\n<p><strong>Sympathetic stimulation is the main way to raise contractility.</strong> Sympathetic nerves release <strong>noradrenaline</strong> onto the heart, and the adrenal medulla releases <strong>adrenaline</strong> into the blood. Both act on <strong>β₁ receptors</strong> on heart muscle cells:</p>\n<div class=\"co-cause-flow\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Beta adrenergic inotropic pathway\"><span>β₁ receptor</span><b>→</b><span>Gs / adenylyl cyclase</span><b>→</b><span>cAMP / PKA ↑</span><b>→</b><span>Ca²⁺ cycling ↑</span><b>→</b><span>Stronger contraction</span></div>\n\n<p>In plain terms, the signal ends up letting <strong>more calcium in</strong> through L-type channels and <strong>more calcium out of the <span data-term=\"sarcoplasmic-reticulum\">sarcoplasmic reticulum</span></strong>. More calcium means more cross-bridges and a stronger beat (positive <strong><span data-term=\"inotropy\">inotropy</span></strong>). The same signal also speeds the pumping of calcium back into storage, so the muscle <strong>relaxes faster</strong> too (positive <strong><span data-term=\"lusitropy\">lusitropy</span></strong>), which helps the ventricle refill at fast heart rates.</p>\n<p><strong>Caffeine and theophylline (<span data-term=\"xanthines\">xanthines</span>)</strong>, which the lecture lists, slow the breakdown of cAMP, so they can strengthen contraction. In everyday doses the effect is small.</p>\n<p><strong>The vagus nerve has little direct effect on ventricular strength.</strong> It mainly slows heart rate and AV conduction. Vagal nerve endings reach the atria and the nodes, but hardly reach the ventricular muscle.</p>\n<p><strong>What weakens contraction?</strong> The lecture lists <strong>too much CO₂ (hypercapnia), too little oxygen (hypoxia), acidosis, some drugs and heart failure</strong>. Low oxygen and acid both interfere with the cell's energy supply and its calcium handling, so each beat is weaker.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> One lecture slide reverses the usual sources. Most of the noradrenaline reaching the heart comes from sympathetic nerves, while circulating adrenaline comes mainly from the adrenal medulla.</aside>\n\n<h2 id=\"8-how-do-preload-afterload-and-inotropy-differ-in-real-situations\"><span class=\"sec-num\">8.</span> How do preload, afterload and inotropy differ in real situations?</h2>\n<div class=\"co-compare-grid\" role=\"group\" aria-label=\"Three ways stroke volume changes\"><div><strong>More venous return</strong><span>Preload ↑</span><small>Often moves <em>along</em> the curve → SV ↑</small></div><div><strong>Higher aortic pressure</strong><span>Afterload ↑</span><small>Ejection becomes harder → SV may ↓</small></div><div><strong>β₁ stimulation</strong><span>Contractility ↑</span><small>More force at same EDV → SV ↑</small></div></div>\n\n<p>Three patients show why each factor must be thought about separately:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Blood loss:</strong> heart rate rises, but there is so little blood returning (low <span data-term=\"preload\">preload</span>) that SV and CO can still fall.</li>\n<li><strong>Aortic stenosis:</strong> even a strong contraction has to force blood through a narrow valve, a high load against ejection.</li>\n<li><strong>Heart failure:</strong> the curve is flat, so extra filling raises pressure and congestion more than SV.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"9-what-happens-to-cardiac-output-during-exercise-and-other-states\"><span class=\"sec-num\">9.</span> What happens to cardiac output during exercise and other states?</h2>\n<p><strong>In exercise, everything pushes cardiac output up at once:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sympathetic nerves raise <strong>heart rate</strong> and <strong>contractility</strong>.</li>\n<li>Contracting muscles and deeper breathing push more blood back to the heart (<strong>more venous return</strong>), so preload rises.</li>\n<li>Blood vessels in active muscle widen, lowering resistance so blood flows out more easily.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>So both HR and SV rise, and CO can increase several-fold.</p>\n<aside class=\"source-note\"><strong>Source note:</strong> One lecture slide shows a downward arrow for venous return during exercise. The lecture's own diagrams, and standard physiology, show that the muscle and breathing pumps <strong>increase</strong> venous return in exercise.</aside>\n\n<p>The lecture lists other states that raise cardiac output: <strong>eating, a hot environment, pregnancy, anxiety and excitement, adrenaline-like drugs and an overactive thyroid (thyrotoxicosis)</strong>. Each works through different mechanisms.</p>\n<p><strong>Blood loss (link to shock):</strong> after a large bleed, venous return and preload fall. Reflex sympathetic activity raises heart rate and tightens blood vessels to hold up blood pressure for a while, but if SV falls far enough, CO and tissue blood flow still fall. <em>(<a href=\"#/physiology/cvs/shock\">→ Shock</a>)</em></p>\n<section class=\"check-block\"><button type=\"button\" class=\"check-launch\" aria-haspopup=\"dialog\" data-questions=\"5\" data-part=\"Sections 6–9\"><span class=\"cl-badge\" aria-hidden=\"true\">?</span><span class=\"cl-text\"><strong>Understanding Check</strong><span class=\"cl-sub\">Sections 6–9 · 5 questions</span></span><span class=\"cl-go\">Start</span></button><div class=\"check-body\"><p class=\"check-label\"><strong>Understanding Check: Sections 6–9</strong></p>\n<p><strong>1.</strong> Which change would lower end-diastolic volume?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Contracting leg muscles while walking briskly</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. A slower heart rate that lengthens diastole</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Fluid around the heart pressing on the ventricles</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Veins tightening under sympathetic stimulation</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cardiac-output:90eee97c\" data-answer=\"C\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: C.</em> Fluid in the pericardium (tamponade) presses on the heart from outside, so the ventricles can't expand and fill. The other three all increase filling.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>2.</strong> How does sympathetic stimulation make each contraction stronger?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. It stretches the ventricle so filaments overlap better</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. It raises cAMP, so more calcium reaches the filaments</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. It slows the heart, giving more time for filling</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. It lowers aortic pressure, making ejection easier</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cardiac-output:b3767fe5\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> β₁ stimulation raises cAMP, which increases calcium entry and release. More calcium means more cross-bridges and a stronger beat at the same stretch.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>3.</strong> A patient in severe heart failure is given a large volume of fluid. Why might their SV barely rise?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Their ventricle sits on a flat part of a low curve</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Fluid lowers their heart rate and so their output</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Extra fluid raises their contractility too much</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Fluid reduces afterload, so the ventricle empties early</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cardiac-output:7ba04475\" data-answer=\"A\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: A.</em> On a flat, low curve, extra filling adds little SV but raises the pressure behind the heart, worsening congestion.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>4.</strong> Why does dynamic exercise increase venous return?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. Gravity drains blood from the legs more easily during exercise</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. The vagus nerve opens the venous valves during exercise</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Lower heart rate gives the veins more time to empty</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. Contracting muscles and deep breathing pump blood back to the heart</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cardiac-output:a68b78ef\" data-answer=\"D\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: D.</em> Muscle contractions squeeze the veins (valves keep the flow heading to the heart), and deeper breathing draws blood into the chest.</p></details>\n\n<p><strong>5.</strong> After a large bleed, a patient's heart rate is 130 beats/min, yet their tissues are poorly perfused. What is the best explanation?<span class=\"mcq-options\"><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"A\">A. A high heart rate always reduces cardiac output</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"B\">B. Low preload cut SV so much that faster beating could not make up for it</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"C\">C. Sympathetic stimulation weakens contraction after blood loss</span><span class=\"mcq-option\" data-option=\"D\">D. The extra beats raise afterload, so no blood is ejected</span></span></p>\n<details class=\"mcq-answer\" data-qid=\"cardiac-output:a1e32b2f\" data-answer=\"B\"><summary>Show answer and explanation</summary><p><em>Answer: B.</em> Blood loss reduces venous return and EDV, so SV falls. Since CO = HR × SV, a large enough fall in SV beats the rise in HR.</p></details></div></section>\n<h2 id=\"10-chapter-essentials\"><span class=\"sec-num\">10.</span> Chapter Essentials</h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>CO = HR × SV</strong>, about 5–5.5 L/min at rest. Right and left outputs must be equal over time (in series).</li>\n<li><strong>Cardiac index</strong> = CO ÷ body surface area (L/min/m²).</li>\n<li><strong>Heart rate alone doesn't predict CO.</strong> Very fast rates shorten filling and can lower SV enough to drop CO.</li>\n<li><strong>SV = EDV − ESV</strong>, set by preload, afterload and contractility.</li>\n<li><strong>Preload</strong> = the stretch before contraction (estimated by EDV). <strong>Afterload</strong> = what the ventricle pushes against (mainly aortic pressure). <strong>Contractility</strong> = strength at the same stretch.</li>\n<li><strong>Frank–Starling:</strong> more filling → more stretch → better filament overlap and calcium sensitivity → more SV. It matches output to input and keeps the two ventricles in step.</li>\n<li><strong>Along the curve</strong> = preload change (heterometric). <strong>To a new curve</strong> = contractility change (homometric). Afterload also shifts curves.</li>\n<li><strong>EDV rises</strong> with more blood volume, venous tone, muscle and breathing pumps, atrial contraction and longer filling. <strong>It falls</strong> with blood loss, standing, tamponade, stiff ventricles and very fast rates.</li>\n<li><strong>Sympathetic β₁ → cAMP → more calcium</strong> → stronger contraction and faster relaxation. The vagus mainly slows the heart and barely weakens the ventricles.</li>\n<li><strong>Hypoxia, hypercapnia, acidosis, some drugs and heart failure</strong> weaken contraction.</li>\n<li><strong>Exercise</strong> raises HR, contractility and venous return together. Blood loss lowers preload, and tachycardia may not save CO.</li>\n</ol>\n";
